## Why does one number cost 1,280 reads?

Tigris keeps all of its metadata in FoundationDB: tenants, buckets, IAM,
billing, and object rows. Every metadata operation is an ACID transaction, and
that includes usage accounting. Every PUT, DELETE, and multipart completion
updates a set of counters for its bucket: row counts, plus byte sizes per
storage tier.

Here is the catch. If each counter were a single key, every write to a hot
bucket would fight over the same few keys, and the bucket would serialize on its
own bookkeeping. So each counter is sharded across 128 keys instead, and a write
bumps one shard atomically. Writes scale beautifully.

Reads pay the bill. Reconstructing one bucket's size means reading every shard
of every counter, which comes to roughly 10 counters × 128 shards ≈

<Neon>1,280 reads</Neon> for one number on one bucket.

Now multiply that by the dashboard. A tenant with 500 buckets triggers 500 ×
1,280 ≈ <Neon>640,000 FoundationDB reads per page load</Neon>. At our partner's
call volume, this one path dominated list-buckets latency platform-wide.

