## So why not just make it faster?

The obvious fixes attack the compute path: make the scan faster, or precompute
the total into one key. But the compute path is not the problem. The problem is
recomputing values that have not changed.

What made a cache safe here? The answer is a property the metric already had:

<Neon>a bucket size is an approximation the moment it's computed</Neon>. Writes
race the counter scan, replication lags, and staged bytes come and go.
"Approximately right, as of ten minutes ago" is the same promise as
"approximately right, as of now."

That insight did real design work. An early draft added a `with_cached_size`
opt-in flag to the list-buckets request, plus a "force fresh" escape hatch.
Review killed both, because if the number is approximate either way, a knob
choosing which approximation is API surface without a purpose. So the cache
became invisible. Callers change nothing, and a per-namespace feature flag turns
it on.

## Where does the cache live?

Two services matter here. The gateway is the edge service that terminates every
S3 and dashboard request, and the backend is the metadata service that owns
FoundationDB and its counters. The cache lives at the gateway, in front of the
backend. We already run a regional edge cache there: KVRocks, a RocksDB-backed
store that speaks the Redis protocol, shared by every gateway instance in the
region. The bucket-size cache is a new table in an existing store, so it
required no new infrastructure.

The read path has three steps.

<MermaidFrame
  style={{ maxWidth: "40rem", margin: "1.5rem auto" }}
  caption="Most lookups turn around at the edge cache. Only the misses pay the 1,280-read scan."
>
  <CacheFlowDiagram />
</MermaidFrame>

First, the gateway asks the backend for the bucket list _without_ sizes, which
is one cheap range read. Second, it asks the cache for those 500 sizes, and most
of them are already known. Third, it asks the backend to size only the misses,
using a single RPC that names just those buckets. We call this request the
miss-fill, and the gateway caches its results.

Each cached entry gets a TTL of about four days, with a random jitter so that
entries filled together do not all expire together. That `names` filter is the
only API schema change in the whole project.

Why does the bulk miss-fill matter? A naive cache fires one backend call per
missed bucket, so fifty misses would mean fifty RPCs. Ours fires one RPC per
page. And because the cached value is the full `BucketMetadata` message rather
than a hand-projected size struct, there is no second schema to keep in sync. On
a hit, a helper grafts the cached size fields onto the fresh listing.

## How do you trust a four-day-old number?

This is the real engineering. How do you promise a number that old is never
_meaningfully_ wrong?

The answer has two layers, and the elegant part is what we didn't build: there
is no invalidation queue, no tombstone table, and no per-bucket cooldown record.
Nothing durable exists beyond the cache entry itself.

<Takeaway
  n={1}
  title="A write shortens the entry's life. It doesn't delete it."
>
  When a size-changing write lands, whether a PUT, a delete, a copy, a rename,
  or a multipart completion, the gateway shortens the cached entry's remaining
  TTL to 15 minutes, and it does so only if the remaining TTL is currently
  longer. The remaining TTL _is_ the cooldown clock. A long TTL means the entry
  was not invalidated recently, so the gateway shortens it. A short TTL means
  the entry is already dying, so the gateway does nothing. One conditional
  expire encodes the whole rate limiter, idempotently, across every pod. (One
  wrinkle: our deployed KVRocks version lacks `EXPIRE ... LT`, so the shorten is
  a TTL read plus a conditional `Expire`. The property survived. The one-liner
  didn't.)
</Takeaway>

<MermaidFrame
  style={{ maxWidth: "40rem", margin: "1.5rem auto" }}
  caption="A write does not evict the entry. It gives the entry 15 minutes to live, and extra writes cannot shorten it further."
>
  <CooldownDiagram />
</MermaidFrame>

<Takeaway n={2} title="A per-pod debounce absorbs the storm.">
  A bucket taking 100 writes per second would otherwise fire 100 cache
  operations per second. A small in-memory LRU list keyed by bucket collapses
  that to roughly one operation per cooldown window, per pod, and the suppressed
  writes never even pay a network round-trip. For writes that land in other
  regions, the gateway already receives an invalidation event from our object
  cache, a separate and older cache for object data. The bucket-size cache
  simply rides along on that event, so we added no new event type and changed no
  producer.
</Takeaway>

<MermaidFrame
  style={{ maxWidth: "40rem", margin: "1.5rem auto" }}
  caption="100 writes per second go in. About one cache operation per cooldown window comes out."
>
  <DebounceDiagram />
</MermaidFrame>

Here is the whole mechanism as it runs in the gateway today, both layers,
lightly renamed for print:

```go
// Invalidate marks the bucket's cached size for refresh. Debounced per pod: a write storm
// (or a stream of cross-region notifies) for the same bucket collapses to ~one cache op per
// cooldown, so it never hammers the network.
func (c *BucketSizeCache) Invalidate(ctx context.Context, tenantID, bucket string) {
	if c.recentlyInvalidated(bucket) {
		return
	}
	_ = c.debounce.Set(bucket, c.now())

	shortened, err := c.shortenTTL(ctx, tenantID, bucket)
	switch {
	case err != nil:
		metrics.Invalidation(outcomeError)
	case shortened:
		metrics.Invalidation(outcomeFired)
	default:
		metrics.Invalidation(outcomeSkipped)
	}
}

// The entry's own remaining TTL is the cooldown clock: shorten to the cooldown only
// when currently longer, so a hot bucket isn't re-invalidated on every write (0 deletes).
func (c *BucketSizeCache) shortenTTL(ctx context.Context, tenantID, bucket string) (bool, error) {
	key := c.cacheKey(tenantID, bucket)
	ttl, err := c.cache.TTL(ctx, key)
	if err != nil {
		return false, err
	}
	if ttl <= 0 || ttl <= c.cfg.Cooldown {
		return false, nil
	}
	return true, c.cache.Expire(ctx, key, c.cfg.Cooldown)
}
```

Every outcome is a metric: fired, skipped, or error. The hit rate, the fallback
count, and the age of served values each get their own series, so "is the cache
healthy" is a dashboard rather than a guess.

Together the two layers give you two bounds, and the bounds are the point:

- A busy bucket is at most <Neon>~15 minutes stale</Neon>, and under sustained
  writes it re-converges on that schedule forever.
- A missed invalidation, whether from a dropped event, a bug, or a network
  partition, is bounded by the multi-day TTL. The TTL is not the mechanism. It
  is the insurance.

What happens if the cache itself dies? Nothing dramatic happens. Any cache
failure falls back to the uncached path, so the page costs what it cost before
the cache existed, and a fallback metric counts how often that happens. Mass
expiry stays spread out as well, because that is what the TTL jitter is for. And
rollback is a config flip: disable the namespace flag, and the next call passes
straight through.

## Fifteen ways to be wrong

A multi-day TTL is only safe if you list the ways it can lie to you. The design
doc enumerates fifteen corner cases, and three are worth retelling.

**A bucket is deleted and recreated.** Someone deletes bucket `photos`, then
creates a new `photos`. The hard-delete path emits no cache event (we checked
the code rather than assuming), so the newborn bucket could wear its ancestor's
size for days. The defense is free: the cached value carries the bucket's
`created_at`, the fresh listing carries the new one, and a mismatch counts as a
miss.

**A burst is followed by silence.** A write burst shortens the TTL once, then
the writes stop and no future event will ever fire. Without care, the entry
would serve its pre-burst size for days. Shorten-not-delete absorbs this case:
the entry expires within minutes of the burst no matter what, and the next read
refills it fresh.

**We kept one race.** A miss-fill dispatches at T0. A write lands at T1, while
the fill is in flight. The fill returns at T2 and caches the pre-write size,
under a fresh TTL. We considered generation counters, tombstones, and
compare-and-swap, and we rejected them all. The window is tens of milliseconds,
the staleness ceiling is the same TTL we already accept, and the metric is
approximate by contract. We documented the race and moved on.

## What the spec got wrong

Our design doc has an unusual section: as-built annotations, recording where the
implementation diverged from the spec and why. The diffs are the most
instructive part.

The original design stored a `last_modified` timestamp inside the cached value
as the cooldown anchor, with delete-plus-refill semantics. The implementation
replaced it with the TTL-shorten plus the debounce. The replacement is simpler,
carries one less field, and makes burst-then-silence fall out for free instead
of needing a special clamp.

We also added a tenant-ID field to the cross-region cache event, and one PR
later we reverted it. The namespace is resolvable from the bucket at handle
time, so it does not need to ride every write event in the system. Growing a
hot-path event to save one lookup in a cold handler is the wrong trade, and it
took a reviewer to say so.

Specs are hypotheses. The honest changelog is worth more.

## The arithmetic, after

A warm page pays one metadata range read, a single scan over the ~500 rows, plus
one batched cache lookup. It runs zero counter scans. A cold page adds one bulk
miss-fill for the ~50 buckets the cache doesn't know, and each of those fills is
amortized over days of later hits.

<MermaidFrame
  style={{ maxWidth: "40rem", margin: "1.5rem auto" }}
  caption="A warm page pays only the metadata range read, about three orders of magnitude less."
>
  <CostChartDiagram />
</MermaidFrame>

The chart shows the per-page saving. The fleet-wide target was blunter still:
take roughly <Neon>a million expensive size queries down to about a
hundred</Neon>, which is four orders of magnitude, with staleness provably
bounded at 15 minutes for busy buckets and at the TTL for pathological failures.

The cache will also outlive its own backend. The counter scan behind the
miss-fill is slated for replacement by a dedicated analytical store, and when
that lands, only the miss-fill swaps. The cache table, the TTL-shorten, the
debounce, and the bounds all carry over. Even a fast analytical backend should
not be asked about buckets that have not changed.

## Takeaways

<Takeaway
  n={1}
  title="The design that makes writes scale makes reads expensive."
>
  Sharding a counter doesn't remove the cost of aggregation. It hands the cost
  to whoever reads. Know which side of that trade your traffic lives on before
  your dashboard becomes your most expensive endpoint.
</Takeaway>

<Takeaway n={2} title="An approximate metric is a caching license.">
  If callers can't tell "as of now" from "as of 15 minutes ago," refusing to
  cache is pure waste. Skip the force-fresh knob as well, because nobody needs
  it and API surface is forever.
</Takeaway>

<Takeaway
  n={3}
  title="The cache is not the engineering. The staleness bounds are."
>
  Anyone can put Redis in front of a slow query. The safe part is the
  enumeration: fifteen corner cases, two provable bounds, and one race accepted
  on purpose, written down where the next engineer can check it.
</Takeaway>

